   "text": "I read the drawing as a cyclic peptide with an exocyclic tail. The stereo marks are my best guess from the wedges, and the bottom alanine is the least certain part.\n\n**Exocyclic tail.** A piperidine amide is attached to an alanine, then a Phe whose nitrogen carries a methyl group (N-Me-Phe). The N-Me-Phe nitrogen is bonded to the macrocycle, at the alpha carbon of an aspartic-acid-type residue.\n\n**Macrocycle.** Tracing the ring from that Asp-type alpha carbon:\n1. Its side-chain CH2\u2013C(=O) forms an amide to the nitrogen of N-methylglycine (sarcosine).\n2. Next come N-Me-Leu, Val, N-Me-Leu, Thr, Ala, N-Me-Phe, Phe and N-Me-Leu.\n3. The ring closes back through the Asp-type residue's own NH.\n\n**Uncertain points.** The bottom alanine is drawn as a CH with a hashed H, but I could not clearly see its methyl group. I assumed alanine, since a hashed H implies a fourth substituent. Chirality at each centre follows the wedges and hashes as I read them. I treated the two alanines as D and the other residues as L, which is a guess for several centres.\n\nSMILES: C[C@@H](NC(=O)[C@@H](Cc1ccccc1)N(C)C(=O)[C@H]1CC(=O)N(C)CC(=O)N(C)[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H](C(C)C)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H]([C@@H](C)O)C(=O)N[C@H](C)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)N1)C(=O)N1CCCCC1"
